{
  "projection_type": "oblique",
  "camera_view": { "heightFraction": 0.68 },
  "elements": [
    "3×3 lattice of open white circles (hidden state nodes x), drawn as three receding rows of three sites",
    "Horizontal intra-row edges joining the three white circles of each row, continuing past the rightmost circle to the right frame edge",
    "Short diagonal inter-row edges slanting down-and-left from each white circle of a row to the corresponding circle of the next row forward, continuing off the lower-left corner from the front row",
    "One vertical edge rising from each white circle",
    "One solid gray filled circle (observation node y) at the top of each vertical edge, drawn above and slightly behind its white partner — 9 gray nodes total",
    "Bold labels x1, x2, x3 to the upper-right of the three white circles of the back (labeled) row",
    "Bold labels y1, y2, y3 above-right of the three gray circles attached to the back row",
    "Black outlines on all circles and all edges (uniform stroke weight)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "selectSite",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "The Markov blanket of a site: a state variable is conditionally independent of the whole field given its four lattice neighbors and its own observation."
    },
    {
      "id": "modelLayers",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Model view",
      "options": ["Hidden lattice only", "Observation links only", "Full model"],
      "default": "Full model",
      "teaches": "That the model factorizes into two distinct pieces — neighbor-to-neighbor coupling among hidden states, and one per-site evidence attachment from each observation."
    },
    {
      "id": "coupling",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Pairwise coupling strength β",
      "range": [0, 1, 0.01],
      "default": 0,
      "teaches": "Stronger pairwise potentials drive neighboring hidden states toward equal values, producing spatial smoothness, while the observations stay fixed."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Hidden state lattice",
      "narration": "Only the white x nodes and their grid edges are shown: each site is joined to its four lattice neighbors, and the edges run off frame to signal an unbounded array.",
      "state": { "modelLayers": "Hidden lattice only", "coupling": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Per-site observations",
      "narration": "Now only the vertical links appear: every gray y node hangs off exactly one white x node and touches nothing else, so evidence enters the model one site at a time.",
      "state": { "modelLayers": "Observation links only", "coupling": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Full MRF",
      "narration": "Both layers together form the Markov random field. Click any white node to see its Markov blanket: four neighbors plus its own observation.",
      "state": { "modelLayers": "Full model", "coupling": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Smoothness from coupling",
      "narration": "Raising the coupling strength makes neighboring white nodes take on similar shading while the gray observations stay fixed — pairwise potentials pull the field toward piecewise-constant labelings.",
      "state": { "modelLayers": "Full model", "coupling": 0.85 }
    }
  ],
  "geometry_notes": "Lattice lies in the X–Z plane. +X is screen-right (intra-row direction), +Z is the receding oblique diagonal drawn toward the UPPER-RIGHT on screen (so the back/labeled row is highest and rightmost, and successive rows step DOWN-AND-LEFT toward the viewer, exactly as in the source). +Y is screen-up (observation attachment direction). Site spacing: 2.6 units in X, 2.6 units in Z; the oblique depth axis is drawn at roughly 45° up-right at ~0.55 scale, giving each row a screen offset of about (+0.9, +1.0) relative to the row in front of it. Visible sites: i = 0,1,2 in X and j = 0,1,2 in Z (j=2 is the back labeled row). White x-node: open circle, radius 0.38 world units, fill #FFFFFF, stroke #000000, stroke width ~0.07 (heavy). Gray y-node: filled circle radius 0.40, fill #8C8C8C, stroke #000000 same weight, positioned at the white node's (x,z) but +1.9 in Y and +0.35 in Z (so it reads as slightly behind its partner). Vertical edge from white node top to gray node bottom. Horizontal edges connect (i,j)→(i+1,j) and the rightmost node of each row extends a further 2.8 units in +X to the frame edge. Diagonal edges connect (i,j)→(i,j-1) (down-left on screen); the front row (j=0) extends a further 1.6 units in -Z off the lower-left corner. Labels only on the back row: bold serif 'x1','x2','x3' placed immediately up-and-right of each back-row white circle (offset ~ +0.45X, +0.35Y), and 'y1','y2','y3' up-and-right of each back-row gray circle. Palette: pure black lines and text, white node fill #FFFFFF, gray node fill #8C8C8C, background #FFFFFF. Click highlight: selected node stroke and its four grid edges plus its vertical link in a saturated accent (#1A6FD4); all non-blanket edges and nodes fade to ~15% opacity (#CCCCCC). Coupling slider model: give each site a latent value s(i,j) initialized from fixed per-site 'observations' o(i,j) in [0,1] (use a fixed pseudo-random seed so the pattern is stable); render white-node fill as a grayscale ramp from #FFFFFF (0) to #4D4D4D (1) of the smoothed value s = (1-β)·o + β·(mean of 4-neighbor o), iterated ~8 times as β rises so that at β=1 neighboring nodes converge to nearly identical shading; gray y-node fills always encode the unchanged o(i,j) with the same ramp but remain visually distinct (they keep a darker base tone).",
  "notes": "Render with an orthographic camera and a fixed oblique shear applied to the depth axis (project world (x,y,z) → screen (x + 0.55·z·cos45°, y + 0.55·z·sin45°)) rather than an orbiting perspective camera; no orbit control, the view stays locked so the figure matches the source. Draw nodes as flat circles (CircleGeometry or sprites) that always face the camera, with a slightly larger black circle behind each for the outline — cheaper and crisper than tube outlines. Edges as Line2/LineMaterial fat lines (linewidth ~3px) so weight is uniform regardless of depth; draw edges before nodes and rely on renderOrder rather than depth testing so nodes occlude edge endpoints cleanly. Off-frame edge continuations should be clipped by the frame, not faded. Labels via CSS2DRenderer HTML in bold serif with subscripts (x<sub>1</sub>), anchored to the back-row node positions. Fades and highlight colors animated with a short (~200 ms) lerp on material color/opacity. Toggle hides edge groups by setting group.visible, keeping node positions fixed so the learner sees the same lattice throughout."
}

