CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a line drawing of a graphical model laid out as a two-dimensional lattice in oblique projection, built from two kinds of nodes. Open white circles occupy the lattice sites, arranged in three receding rows of three visible sites each; within a row they are joined by horizontal edges that run past the rightmost circle to the frame edge, and between rows by short diagonal edges slanting down and to the left, which likewise continue off the lower-left corner, so the grid reads as a fragment of an unbounded array. A single vertical edge rises from each white circle to a solid gray filled circle, one gray node per site, drawn above and slightly behind its partner. Only the back row carries labels: its white circles are marked with the boldface symbols x_1, x_2, and x_3, and the gray circles directly above them y_1, y_2, and y_3. Because every gray node hangs off exactly one white node while the white nodes alone form the grid, the drawing separates per-site attachments from neighbor-to-neighbor coupling. The structure depicts a Markov random field over a spatial grid, each site carrying a state variable tied to its four lattice neighbors.

PROJECTION TYPE: oblique
Call setCameraView({ projection: 'oblique', obliqueAngleDeg: 45, depthScale: 0.5, heightFraction }) after all geometry is added — a standard cabinet-oblique pose; the plan does not measure per-figure oblique angles. The scaffold implements oblique as a shear of the eye-space projection matrix, so the front face stays undistorted, depth edges leave at obliqueAngleDeg, and labels track the shear automatically. Build the TRUE 3D object — the shear is the camera's job. Do NOT shear geometry, do NOT wrap the figure in a sheared parent Object3D, and do NOT disable automatic matrix updates. The scaffold also provides the reveal interaction: dragging eases the shear out to expose the real 3D shape and a toggle button returns to the printed view — do not implement either yourself.
AXIS ASSIGNMENT: the pose is pinned, so in BoxGeometry(w, h, d) the w axis is screen-right, h is screen-up, and d is the receding diagonal. Before writing each BoxGeometry, look at the source image and decide which of the object's dimensions actually recedes there — do not assume it is the one the domain calls "depth". In feature-map / layer-stack diagrams the channel count is usually the w (horizontal) extent along the flow direction and a spatial dimension is d. Sanity-check the silhouette against the source: a block that should read as a long bar pointing at its neighbours must be long in w, not in d, or it will render as a sliver shooting into the page. If GEOMETRY NOTES assigns the axes in a way that contradicts the source image's silhouette, follow the image.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - 3×3 lattice of open white circles (hidden state nodes x), drawn as three receding rows of three sites
  - Horizontal intra-row edges joining the three white circles of each row, continuing past the rightmost circle to the right frame edge
  - Short diagonal inter-row edges slanting down-and-left from each white circle of a row to the corresponding circle of the next row forward, continuing off the lower-left corner from the front row
  - One vertical edge rising from each white circle
  - One solid gray filled circle (observation node y) at the top of each vertical edge, drawn above and slightly behind its white partner — 9 gray nodes total
  - Bold labels x1, x2, x3 to the upper-right of the three white circles of the back (labeled) row
  - Bold labels y1, y2, y3 above-right of the three gray circles attached to the back row
  - Black outlines on all circles and all edges (uniform stroke weight)

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
Lattice lies in the X–Z plane. +X is screen-right (intra-row direction), +Z is the receding oblique diagonal drawn toward the UPPER-RIGHT on screen (so the back/labeled row is highest and rightmost, and successive rows step DOWN-AND-LEFT toward the viewer, exactly as in the source). +Y is screen-up (observation attachment direction). Site spacing: 2.6 units in X, 2.6 units in Z; the oblique depth axis is drawn at roughly 45° up-right at ~0.55 scale, giving each row a screen offset of about (+0.9, +1.0) relative to the row in front of it. Visible sites: i = 0,1,2 in X and j = 0,1,2 in Z (j=2 is the back labeled row). White x-node: open circle, radius 0.38 world units, fill #FFFFFF, stroke #000000, stroke width ~0.07 (heavy). Gray y-node: filled circle radius 0.40, fill #8C8C8C, stroke #000000 same weight, positioned at the white node's (x,z) but +1.9 in Y and +0.35 in Z (so it reads as slightly behind its partner). Vertical edge from white node top to gray node bottom. Horizontal edges connect (i,j)→(i+1,j) and the rightmost node of each row extends a further 2.8 units in +X to the frame edge. Diagonal edges connect (i,j)→(i,j-1) (down-left on screen); the front row (j=0) extends a further 1.6 units in -Z off the lower-left corner. Labels only on the back row: bold serif 'x1','x2','x3' placed immediately up-and-right of each back-row white circle (offset ~ +0.45X, +0.35Y), and 'y1','y2','y3' up-and-right of each back-row gray circle. Palette: pure black lines and text, white node fill #FFFFFF, gray node fill #8C8C8C, background #FFFFFF. Click highlight: selected node stroke and its four grid edges plus its vertical link in a saturated accent (#1A6FD4); all non-blanket edges and nodes fade to ~15% opacity (#CCCCCC). Coupling slider model: give each site a latent value s(i,j) initialized from fixed per-site 'observations' o(i,j) in [0,1] (use a fixed pseudo-random seed so the pattern is stable); render white-node fill as a grayscale ramp from #FFFFFF (0) to #4D4D4D (1) of the smoothed value s = (1-β)·o + β·(mean of 4-neighbor o), iterated ~8 times as β rises so that at β=1 neighboring nodes converge to nearly identical shading; gray y-node fills always encode the unchanged o(i,j) with the same ramp but remain visually distinct (they keep a darker base tone).

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
