      "coupling": 0
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Smoothness from coupling",
    "narration": "Raising the coupling strength makes neighboring white nodes take on similar shading while the gray observations stay fixed — pairwise potentials pull the field toward piecewise-constant labelings.",
    "state": {
      "modelLayers": "Full model",
      "coupling": 0.85
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "heightFraction": 0.68
}

NOTES: Render with an orthographic camera and a fixed oblique shear applied to the depth axis (project world (x,y,z) → screen (x + 0.55·z·cos45°, y + 0.55·z·sin45°)) rather than an orbiting perspective camera; no orbit control, the view stays locked so the figure matches the source. Draw nodes as flat circles (CircleGeometry or sprites) that always face the camera, with a slightly larger black circle behind each for the outline — cheaper and crisper than tube outlines. Edges as Line2/LineMaterial fat lines (linewidth ~3px) so weight is uniform regardless of depth; draw edges before nodes and rely on renderOrder rather than depth testing so nodes occlude edge endpoints cleanly. Off-frame edge continuations should be clipped by the frame, not faded. Labels via CSS2DRenderer HTML in bold serif with subscripts (x<sub>1</sub>), anchored to the back-row node positions. Fades and highlight colors animated with a short (~200 ms) lerp on material color/opacity. Toggle hides edge groups by setting group.visible, keeping node positions fixed so the learner sees the same lattice throughout.

